--- body ---
## Context
Child of ticket 1099 (bound review time), carrying its exit criterion 3, a measurement that can only
be taken after 1099 lands: 1099 adds `scripts/seat_clock.py` (a 600 s cap on headless readers and a
`roster` of each seat's wall time or failure) and the `seat times:` line of the `/gaze` result comment.
The author's window is 20 minutes from first review to merge; raids 1080 and 1089 took 79 and 59
(conception/discussions/2026-10-14-1096-design.md, "What the evidence says about the 20-minute window").
Not blocked by 1099 in the DAG: it simply has nothing to measure until 1099 is merged.

## How to measure
Take the next raid (or hunt) after 1099 merges that runs `/gaze` with a panel. For one of its PRs, from
the forge timestamps and the posted `/gaze` result comments, write the timeline: first review start,
each `/gaze` round (its `seat times:` line, i.e. `seat_clock.py roster` output, and its `telemetry:
wall=`), each `/verify-gate` round, fix rounds, merge. Attribute every minute to a phase (seat runs,
timed-out or missing seats, retries, fix rounds, gate, waiting); label derived numbers as derived.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] One raid PR's closing time (first review to merge) is broken down by phase from the recorded
      `seat times:` lines and forge timestamps, and reported against the 20-minute window.
- [ ] Every seat that ran appears with its wall time or its failure; a gap in the record is named,
      not filled.
- [ ] No new numerical budget is asserted beyond 1099's 600 s reader cap; the report says whether
      the window was met and which phase dominated.
